-- status  : Open
-- author  : @mikedeng1
-- created : 2026-10-09T20:14:17.887367+00:00
-- url     : https://prove2.me/theorems/b8a54170-73ee-454c-9994-7ca5ccaab88b
-- title:
--   Theorem 5.1, pp. 18–19 — for Φ ∈ 𝚷_{n,T}, data are informative for quadratic stabilization iff (5.5) is feasible; under Φ₂₂ < 0 and full row rank, iff (5.6) is; K = LP⁻¹ stabilizes all of Σ
-- statement:
--   Let $\Phi\in\boldsymbol\Pi_{n,T}$ and consider data $(U_-,X)$ generated by $x(t+1)=A_sx(t)+B_su(t)+w(t)$ with noise satisfying (2.3). Write
--   $$\mathcal L_\beta(P,L)=\begin{bmatrix}P-\beta I&0&0&0\\0&-P&-L^\top&0\\0&-L&0&L\\0&0&L^\top&P\end{bmatrix}-\begin{bmatrix}I&X_+\\0&-X_-\\0&-U_-\\0&0\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}\Phi_{11}&\Phi_{12}\\ \Phi_{21}&\Phi_{22}\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}I&X_+\\0&-X_-\\0&-U_-\\0&0\end{bmatrix}^{\!\top}.$$
--
--   1. **(a)** The data are informative for quadratic stabilization if and only if there exist $P\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}$, $P>0$, $L\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}$ and $\beta>0$ with
--   $$\mathcal L_\beta(P,L)\geqslant 0. \tag{5.5}$$
--   2. **(b)** If in addition $\Phi_{22}<0$ and $\operatorname{rank}\begin{bmatrix}X_-\\U_-\end{bmatrix}=n+m$, the data are informative for quadratic stabilization if and only if there exist $P>0$ and $L\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}$ with
--   $$\mathcal L_0(P,L)>0. \tag{5.6}$$
--   3. **Moreover**, whenever $(P,L,\beta)$ satisfies (5.5), or $(P,L)$ satisfies (5.6), the gain $K:=LP^{-1}$ stabilizes every $(A,B)\in\Sigma$: $P-(A+BK)P(A+BK)^\top>0$ and $A+BK$ is Schur stable.
--
--   This is the main data-driven control result of the paper: a necessary and sufficient LMI test for the existence of a single state-feedback controller, with a common quadratic Lyapunov function, that stabilizes every system consistent with noisy data, under any noise model in the class $\boldsymbol\Pi_{n,T}$ and with no rank or Slater condition in part (a).
--
--   **Formalization Note** "The data are generated by (2.1) with noise model (2.3)" is the hypothesis that some $(A_s,B_s)$ lies in $\Sigma$; it is essential (if $\Sigma=\emptyset$, informativity holds vacuously). $\mathcal L_\beta$ and $\mathcal L_0$ are `lmi55` and `lmi56`; informativity is `InformativeQS` (Definition 2.1); "stabilizing feedback gain for all $(A,B)\in\Sigma$" is `StabilizesAll`, stated with the $P$ of the feasible LMI: the Lyapunov inequality (2.4) together with $(A+BK)^k\to 0$. $P^{-1}$ is Mathlib's matrix inverse, the true inverse since $P>0$. The Moreover part is stated under the theorem's standing hypotheses only, without those of (b), as in the paper. No hypothesis $n\geqslant 1$ is added: at $n=0$ both sides of (a) and (b) hold.
-- source:
--   van Waarde–Camlibel–Eising–Trentelman, arXiv:2203.12959v3, Theorem 5.1, pp. 18–19, (5.5), (5.6)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_QuadMatIneq_Stabilization_QMI
import Definitions.Def_QuadMatIneq_Stabilization_Data

open Matrix Filter Topology

namespace QuadMatIneq.Stabilization

/-- Theorem 5.1, pp. 18–19. `hgen` is "the data `(U₋, X)` are generated by (2.1) with noise
model (2.3)": the true system `(A_s, B_s)` lies in `Σ`. -/
theorem theorem_5_1 {n m T : ℕ}
    (Φ : Matrix (Fin n ⊕ Fin T) (Fin n ⊕ Fin T) ℝ) (X : Matrix (Fin n) (Fin (T + 1)) ℝ)
    (Um : Matrix (Fin m) (Fin T) ℝ) (hΦ : InPi Φ)
    (hgen : ∃ As : Matrix (Fin n) (Fin n) ℝ, ∃ Bs : Matrix (Fin n) (Fin m) ℝ,
      (As, Bs) ∈ Sigma Φ X Um) :
    -- (a)
    (InformativeQS Φ X Um ↔
      ∃ P : Matrix (Fin n) (Fin n) ℝ, ∃ L : Matrix (Fin m) (Fin n) ℝ, ∃ β : ℝ,
        P.PosDef ∧ 0 < β ∧ (lmi55 Φ X Um P L β).PosSemidef) ∧
    -- (b)
    ((-Φ.toBlocks₂₂).PosDef → (Matrix.fromRows (Xminus X) Um).rank = n + m →
      (InformativeQS Φ X Um ↔
        ∃ P : Matrix (Fin n) (Fin n) ℝ, ∃ L : Matrix (Fin m) (Fin n) ℝ,
          P.PosDef ∧ (lmi56 Φ X Um P L).PosDef)) ∧
    -- Moreover, for (5.5)
    (∀ (P : Matrix (Fin n) (Fin n) ℝ) (L : Matrix (Fin m) (Fin n) ℝ) (β : ℝ),
      P.PosDef → 0 < β → (lmi55 Φ X Um P L β).PosSemidef → StabilizesAll Φ X Um P (L * P⁻¹)) ∧
    -- Moreover, for (5.6)
    (∀ (P : Matrix (Fin n) (Fin n) ℝ) (L : Matrix (Fin m) (Fin n) ℝ),
      P.PosDef → (lmi56 Φ X Um P L).PosDef → StabilizesAll Φ X Um P (L * P⁻¹)) := by sorry

end QuadMatIneq.Stabilization
